<p>Sometimes you find that a mode grabs a shortcut you've already defined
for something else, or that you want to override. This could be <code>C-c</code>
shortcuts, which are supposed to be free for the end user to configure
but some mode set anyway, or something else.</p>
<p>Today, I set up an LSP server for my Markdown files and discovered my
code action shortcut <code>M-RET</code> didn't work, but instead inserted another
Markdown list item. To remedy this, I did the following to remove it
from the mode keymap in question:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre><span></span><code><span class="p">(</span><span class="nb">use-package</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nv">markdown-mode</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="ss">:bind</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="ss">:map</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nv">markdown-mode-map</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s">"M-RET"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="o">.</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="no">nil</span><span class="p">)))</span>
</code></pre></div>

<p>With that, using LSP servers in Markdown is just as smooth as in other
modes. To remedy an LSP marked problem, I hit <code>M-RET</code> and <code>Tab</code>
complete the action I want.</p>
<p>Happy writing!</p>
<h2>PS:</h2>
<p>If you're not using <code>use-package</code>, you can do:</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre><span></span><code><span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">with-eval-after-load</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="ss">'markdown-mode</span>
<span class="w">  </span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nv">keymap-unset</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="nv">markdown-mode-map</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="s">"M-RET"</span><span class="w"> </span><span class="no">t</span><span class="p">))</span>
</code></pre></div>